<section class="faq">
  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

  <h3>How many calories should I eat to lose weight?</h3>
  <p>A common starting point is eating 500 fewer calories than your TDEE per day. That creates a deficit of about 3500 calories per week, which corresponds to roughly 0.5 kg or 1 lb of fat loss per week. This is widely considered a sustainable rate. Going much faster often leads to muscle loss and metabolic slowdown. This calculator shows your personal maintenance number so you know exactly where to start.</p>

  <h3>What formula does this calorie calculator use?</h3>
  <p>It uses the Mifflin-St Jeor equation to calculate BMR. This formula is widely regarded as the most accurate for typical adults. BMR is then multiplied by an activity factor (called the Harris-Benedict activity multiplier) to give your TDEE, which is the total calories your body burns each day including movement and exercise.</p>

  <h3>What is the difference between BMR and TDEE?</h3>
  <p>BMR is the number of calories your body needs to keep basic functions running at complete rest. TDEE is your total daily energy expenditure including movement, exercise, and the energy cost of daily activities. TDEE is always higher than BMR. Most people should base their calorie targets on TDEE not BMR, because nobody actually spends the whole day lying still.</p>

  <h3>Is 1200 calories too low?</h3>
  <p>For most adults 1200 calories per day is below the minimum recommended intake and can lead to muscle loss, nutrient deficiencies, and metabolic adaptation over time. Most women should aim for at least 1400 to 1500 calories for safe fat loss. Men generally need considerably more. This calculator gives you a personalised baseline to work from.</p>

  <h3>How accurate is this calculator?</h3>
  <p>Calorie calculators are estimates, not exact measurements. Individual metabolism varies by about 10 to 15 percent from formulas like Mifflin-St Jeor. Use the result as a starting point, track your actual body weight for two to three weeks, and adjust your intake by 100 to 200 calories if you are not seeing the expected results.</p>
</section>

